Why token cost can point the wrong way

Tokens remain useful for measuring work, but they do not all create equal customer value. A long prompt adds prefill work and a larger KV cache. A long answer adds repeated decode steps. One model may need more tokens, retries or tool calls to finish the same task.

Batching creates another trap. Lesson 057 showed that a larger batch can lower hardware cost per token by raising throughput. If the added queue delay breaks the response-time promise, some of those cheaper tokens no longer belong in the useful-output denominator. Google Cloud describes an inference efficient frontier: for a fixed compute budget, operators trade latency against throughput unless better hardware, algorithms or scheduling move the frontier outward.

Model architecture changes both sides of the fraction. A sparse model may use less arithmetic for each token, as Lesson 058 explained, yet require more accelerators to hold its experts or more communication to route tokens. A smaller model may cost less to run but fail more tasks. A larger model may be cheaper per successful task if its higher acceptance rate avoids retries and review. None of these outcomes is automatic; they must be measured on the same workload.

Prediction check

System A lowers cost per token by 30%, but users ask it to retry twice as often. Has cost per useful output improved?

Answer: Not enough information. We need tokens per attempt, acceptance rates after retries, latency and the full cost boundary. The tempting mistake is to treat a cheaper unit of computation as a cheaper completed job. If extra attempts consume more tokens or still fail the acceptance rule, the product may cost more despite the lower token price.

Measure the complete service

An operator can compare systems in five steps: choose a representative workload; define quality and latency requirements; run each candidate at realistic traffic; count accepted outputs; and divide fully loaded cost by that count. Report quality, tail latency and throughput beside the cost so a team can see which constraint changed.

For an investor, falling cost per token can expand AI usage, but it can also reduce the hardware required for one task. Total infrastructure demand depends on both effects: resources per useful output multiplied by the number of useful outputs customers want. Suppliers capture value when they remove a binding constraint in that equation and retain bargaining power, not merely when their component appears in every server.

The reusable rule is simple: measure the result the customer accepts, then trace every model, software and physical-system cost required to produce it.

Sources

Google Cloud, Five Techniques to Reach the Efficient Frontier of LLM Inference — latency, throughput and compute-budget tradeoffs: https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/five-techniques-to-reach-the-efficient-frontier-of-llm-inference

Google Cloud, Selecting GPUs for LLM Serving on GKE — serving throughput within a latency bound: https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/selecting-gpus-for-llm-serving-on-gke/

MLCommons, MLPerf Inference documentation — accuracy, performance scenarios and latency constraints: https://docs.mlcommons.org/inference/

OpenAI, Evaluation Best Practices — task-specific evaluations and representative test data: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/evaluation-best-practices

Liang et al., Holistic Evaluation of Language Models — multi-scenario, multi-metric model evaluation: https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09110

